{"name":"Cocktail Olive","hex":"#9fa36c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cocktail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixed alcoholic beverage.","A mixture of other substances or things."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Ostentatiously lacking in manners."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cocktail"},{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cocktail","blocks":[{"text":"A cocktail is a mixed drink, usually alcoholic. Most commonly, a cocktail is a combination of one or more spirits mixed with other ingredients, such as juices, flavored syrups, tonic water, shrubs, and bitters. Cocktails vary widely across regions of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0084.jpg/330px-15-09-26-RalfR-WLC-0084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocktail","wikidata":"Q134768"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg/500px-Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg/500px-Cocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cocktail accessories dating back to the 4th century BCE. Museum of the Royal Tombs of Aigai, Greece","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACocktail_accessories_-_Aigai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg/500px-The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg/500px-The_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Queen Mary, a North American cocktail, made by combining beer, grenadine and Maraschino cherries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Official_Queen_Mary_Cocktail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/The_first_definition_of_Cocktail.jpg/500px-The_first_definition_of_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content